  "imageName": "textformat.size.smaller",
  "intro": "Russian adjectives have two forms: long (full) and short. Short adjectives are used only as predicates (after the subject) and don't decline for case - only for gender and number. They often express temporary states or opinions: Он болен. (He is sick.) Short forms are common with certain adjectives: рад (glad), готов (ready), должен (must), нужен (needed). Some adjectives only exist in short form!",
  "rules": [
    "Formation: remove the long ending and add: -∅ (masc), -а (fem), -о (neut), -ы/-и (plural).",
    "Examples: новый → нов/нова/ново/новы; красивый → красив/красива/красиво/красивы.",
    "Fleeting vowel: many adjectives insert о/е in masculine: больной → болен, нужный → нужен.",
    "Common short-only: рад/рада/рады (glad), готов/готова/готовы (ready), должен/должна/должны (must).",
    "IMPORTANT: Before a noun, ALWAYS use the long form: красивая девушка. Short forms are ONLY used after the subject as predicates: Она красива."
  ],
  "examples": [
    "Я рад тебя видеть. (I'm glad to see you.), short only",
    "Она готова. (She is ready.), feminine",
    "Дом очень красив. (The house is very beautiful.), masculine",
    "Дети должны учиться. (Children must study.), plural"
  ],
